1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:04,080
For centuries, the shame of a nation.

2
00:00:05,100 --> 00:00:07,460
Now a symbol of national pride.

3
00:00:08,120 --> 00:00:10,960
This imposing mass of stones and buried bones

4
00:00:10,961 --> 00:00:14,740
is a monument to the thin line between vision and paranoia,

5
00:00:15,320 --> 00:00:17,100
between industriousness and madness.

6
00:00:18,220 --> 00:00:21,480
Now a wonder of the ancient and the modern world,

7
00:00:21,680 --> 00:00:25,220
the Great Wall of China on Modern Marvels.

8
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:43,400
The Great Wall of China became the unofficial symbol of the Chinese nation in 1972,

9
00:00:44,340 --> 00:00:48,918
when then-President Richard Nixon walked upon it during his tour marking U.S.

10
00:00:48,919 --> 00:00:50,820
diplomatic recognition of China.

11
00:00:50,821 --> 00:00:58,560
I think that you would have to conclude that this is a great wall and that it had to be built by a great people.

12
00:00:58,561 --> 00:01:04,520
But the Chinese themselves have not always taken such pride in the Great Wall.

13
00:01:05,360 --> 00:01:13,160
The traditional Chinese view of the wall was that it was a symbol of oppressive government and military weakness and futility.

14
00:01:13,880 --> 00:01:19,120
It was a symbol of the suffering of ordinary Chinese people under despotic rule.

15
00:01:19,121 --> 00:01:29,440
It rises where the mountains meet the sea at Shanghai Kwan and snakes its way across northern China to the edge of the Gobi Desert,

16
00:01:30,140 --> 00:01:36,960
forming the spine of a system of so-called long walls that sprawl over thousands of miles of the Chinese landscape.

17
00:01:39,360 --> 00:01:48,620
Estimates of its length range between 1,500 and 4,000 miles, and its grandeur has been widely celebrated in myth and legend.

18
00:01:49,120 --> 00:01:56,120
Yet the existence of the Great Wall remained unknown to the Western world for more than 1500 years.

19
00:01:56,121 --> 00:02:01,420
Incredibly, it was never depicted in classical Chinese paintings of the period,

20
00:02:01,421 --> 00:02:07,120
nor did Marco Polo mention it in writing about his travels in China in the 13th century.

21
00:02:07,121 --> 00:02:13,020
In fact, the Chinese did not even refer to a Great Wall of China until the 20th century,

22
00:02:13,021 --> 00:02:17,120
after the captivated West gave it a name.

23
00:02:17,121 --> 00:02:26,040
The beginning of the 20th century, you begin to have the Western idea, which glorifies the wall as a sort of symbol of China.

24
00:02:26,041 --> 00:02:35,660
And all of those Western myths are embraced by the Chinese to become part of a new cultural myth for China.

25
00:02:35,661 --> 00:02:49,640
In 1908, author and adventurer William Edgar Giel

26
00:02:49,641 --> 00:02:53,300
became the first American to travel the length of the Great Wall.

27
00:02:53,301 --> 00:02:57,600
His observations were more enthusiastic than illuminating,

28
00:02:57,940 --> 00:03:02,640
and some of his infamous pronouncements have been repeated well into the modern age.

29
00:03:05,660 --> 00:03:08,740
The Great Wall is a symbol of China's golden age.

30
00:03:09,160 --> 00:03:15,040
It is 1,700 miles long and the only man-made structure on Earth visible by eye from the moon.

31
00:03:16,580 --> 00:03:19,360
The Great Wall of China cannot be seen from the moon.

32
00:03:19,720 --> 00:03:24,700
This myth was widespread at the time of the astronauts' flights,

33
00:03:24,820 --> 00:03:26,740
and it's well known in the community of astronauts,

34
00:03:26,880 --> 00:03:31,120
of people who have actually been in space, that you cannot see the Great Wall from outer space.

35
00:03:31,240 --> 00:03:34,420
But astronauts will tell you that nearly every time they go to speak,

36
00:03:34,421 --> 00:03:36,180
someone will ask the question.

37
00:03:37,580 --> 00:03:40,300
Man recently glimpsed the Great Wall from space

38
00:03:40,301 --> 00:03:44,280
with highly enhanced thermographic photographs taken by satellite.

39
00:03:45,200 --> 00:03:49,640
But other durable myths persist, unlikely to be validated by science.

40
00:03:50,900 --> 00:03:54,200
There were all kinds of calculations in the early 19th century

41
00:03:54,201 --> 00:03:56,860
about how you could take all of the stone in the wall

42
00:03:56,861 --> 00:04:00,000
and redeploy it around the equator and build walls there,

43
00:04:00,001 --> 00:04:04,420
or it would be equivalent to all the houses in England and Scotland.

44
00:04:04,540 --> 00:04:07,040
And these are still repeated into the 20th century.

45
00:04:07,700 --> 00:04:12,280
The biggest myth about the Great Wall of China is that there is a Great Wall of China.

46
00:04:12,660 --> 00:04:16,160
It is doubtful that the Great Wall ever existed at all

47
00:04:16,161 --> 00:04:19,800
as a continuous, homogenous barrier across northern China.

48
00:04:20,660 --> 00:04:23,320
Rather, it is a discontinuous series of walls

49
00:04:23,321 --> 00:04:27,280
built at different times by different people for different reasons,

50
00:04:27,281 --> 00:04:34,500
joined together, allowed to decay, rebuilt, and extended over a period of some 2,000 years.

51
00:04:35,400 --> 00:04:38,320
Well, I think when people think of the Great Wall,

52
00:04:38,660 --> 00:04:43,760
they tend to think of this long, sort of monumental, continuous structure

53
00:04:43,761 --> 00:04:47,760
that stretches thousands of miles across the frontier of China.

54
00:04:47,761 --> 00:04:49,860
The reality, of course, is quite different.

55
00:04:50,060 --> 00:04:51,900
The wall was built in pieces.

56
00:04:51,901 --> 00:04:57,180
much of it is nothing more than sort of an archaeological midden of fallen down

57
00:04:57,181 --> 00:05:02,040
ramparts and in desert sections of China much of it's even been buried so the

58
00:05:02,041 --> 00:05:07,740
reality of the wall is not exactly as you see it when you visit Ba Da Ling for

59
00:05:07,741 --> 00:05:12,600
instance outside of Beijing where it's been perfectly restored. Well what we're

60
00:05:12,601 --> 00:05:16,780
referring to when we talk about the Great Wall today is usually what remains

61
00:05:16,781 --> 00:05:21,540
of the walls that were built by the Ming Dynasty the ones that they built in the

62
00:05:21,541 --> 00:05:24,704
16th century are quite remarkable.

63
00:05:24,705 --> 00:05:30,660
They're solid masonry climbing up precipitous mountainsides with

64
00:05:30,661 --> 00:05:36,100
watchtowers and it's really very difficult to imagine how they were built and how they were

65
00:05:36,101 --> 00:05:36,810
completed.

66
00:05:36,811 --> 00:05:42,420
For all its massive size and solid dimensions the Great Wall of China remains a

67
00:05:42,421 --> 00:05:43,759
mysterious presence.

68
00:05:43,760 --> 00:05:48,980
It has never been completely surveyed and even today no one can be sure of its

69
00:05:48,981 --> 00:05:50,572
exact length or route.

70
00:05:50,573 --> 00:05:55,420
The history of the Great Wall that has come down to us is a curious

71
00:05:55,421 --> 00:06:01,900
mixture of reality and imagination, a little solid information interwoven with vast amounts

72
00:06:01,901 --> 00:06:15,901
of myth and tradition, and it is virtually impossible to separate the two.

73
00:06:16,520 --> 00:06:23,060
No other civilization seems to have adopted wall building as enthusiastically as the Chinese.

74
00:06:23,061 --> 00:06:28,580
The first settlements of Neolithic times were surrounded by barriers of pounded earth.

75
00:06:28,581 --> 00:06:32,080
The cornerstone of society was the walled city.

76
00:06:32,081 --> 00:06:39,360
Indeed the Chinese word for city, Chang, also means wall.

77
00:06:39,361 --> 00:06:43,100
China is walls within walls within walls.

78
00:06:43,101 --> 00:06:48,200
The Great Wall was the mother of all walls within which these other concentric circles

79
00:06:48,201 --> 00:06:53,800
of walls, right down to the wall around one's private house, which everybody had.

80
00:06:53,801 --> 00:07:01,540
Walls are very, sort of, deeply organic part of Chinese history and culture.

81
00:07:01,541 --> 00:07:07,320
They surrounded their houses and temples with walls and projected their wall consciousness

82
00:07:07,321 --> 00:07:11,700
beyond the realm of the mortal world into the kingdom of the gods.

83
00:07:11,701 --> 00:07:16,980
The god of walls and moats also had the power over the boundaries between life and death,

84
00:07:16,981 --> 00:07:20,660
being appointed to inform the dying of their fates.

85
00:07:20,661 --> 00:07:26,320
The Chinese built walls to define their spaces, to keep strangers at a distance, and members

86
00:07:26,321 --> 00:07:28,820
of the clan within.

87
00:07:28,821 --> 00:07:32,480
Some walls may have had ritual significance as well.

88
00:07:32,481 --> 00:07:38,000
But ancient China was a hostile realm, and most of the serious walls were probably built

89
00:07:38,001 --> 00:07:39,900
for defensive purposes.

90
00:07:39,901 --> 00:07:42,040
With nomads raiding from the north

91
00:07:42,041 --> 00:07:44,220
and neighboring territories ever alert

92
00:07:44,221 --> 00:07:47,160
for signs of weakness, a wall was considered

93
00:07:47,161 --> 00:07:49,620
a strategic military necessity.

94
00:07:51,300 --> 00:07:53,080
By the end of the eighth century BC,

95
00:07:53,081 --> 00:07:54,640
the land that would become China

96
00:07:54,641 --> 00:07:56,820
had entered a period of interstate strife

97
00:07:56,821 --> 00:07:59,400
that would last for 500 years.

98
00:07:59,401 --> 00:08:01,960
It consisted of dozens of feudal states

99
00:08:01,961 --> 00:08:05,080
and tiny fiefdoms ruled by warlords,

100
00:08:05,081 --> 00:08:07,500
bound loosely to a ruling dynasty

101
00:08:07,501 --> 00:08:11,140
whose power was more spiritual and ritualistic than actual.

102
00:08:12,340 --> 00:08:14,960
By the middle of the 4th century B.C.,

103
00:08:14,961 --> 00:08:19,320
chivalry and allegiance to the king had given way to self-interest and butchery.

104
00:08:19,900 --> 00:08:21,900
It was during this warring states period

105
00:08:21,901 --> 00:08:25,800
that the Chinese began building defensive walls in earnest.

106
00:08:26,920 --> 00:08:29,600
The state of Qi built a wall along its southern border

107
00:08:29,601 --> 00:08:31,200
to keep out the armies of Chu.

108
00:08:31,800 --> 00:08:35,320
Chu built a northern barrier to protect themselves against Qin.

109
00:08:35,321 --> 00:08:40,920
Yan and Chao built walls to keep out northern barbarians and each other.

110
00:08:42,200 --> 00:08:48,320
In all, some 2,800 miles of walls were built along the frontiers of the various fighting states.

111
00:08:51,720 --> 00:08:54,680
At some time, there were about 120 different states.

112
00:08:54,800 --> 00:08:59,680
By the time you get to the height of the warring states period, there are only seven states left.

113
00:09:00,100 --> 00:09:04,380
There has been tremendous destruction of these small independent states throughout China.

114
00:09:05,320 --> 00:09:08,560
Out of the bloodshed and destruction of the warring states period,

115
00:09:08,740 --> 00:09:11,800
the major philosophies of China began to evolve

116
00:09:11,801 --> 00:09:15,780
as learned men tried to figure out what had gone wrong

117
00:09:15,781 --> 00:09:17,400
and how to make it right.

118
00:09:18,160 --> 00:09:21,040
In the 5th century B.C., Confucius stressed the need

119
00:09:21,041 --> 00:09:23,720
for strict observation of the rules and conduct

120
00:09:23,721 --> 00:09:26,240
of relationships between man and heaven

121
00:09:26,241 --> 00:09:29,300
and deplored the necessity for wars and walls.

122
00:09:30,400 --> 00:09:33,860
Another group of thinkers, the Taoists, sought answers in nature

123
00:09:33,861 --> 00:09:36,840
and believed everything to be in a constant state of flux

124
00:09:36,841 --> 00:09:39,040
between the forces of yin and yang.

125
00:09:39,740 --> 00:09:42,100
Therefore, struggle and war were pointless.

126
00:09:43,620 --> 00:09:47,160
Had either of these major philosophies become the state philosophy,

127
00:09:47,360 --> 00:09:50,400
it is unlikely that the Great Wall would ever have been built.

128
00:09:51,080 --> 00:09:53,500
But the state of Qin adopted legalism,

129
00:09:54,060 --> 00:09:58,960
a harsh totalitarian system of rules, punishments, and just rewards.

130
00:09:58,961 --> 00:10:05,960
There's a very nice story about the crown keeper and the coat keeper.

131
00:10:05,961 --> 00:10:09,960
The king falls asleep one night in front of the fire, and the crown keeper comes in and

132
00:10:09,961 --> 00:10:14,060
sees his majesty is cold, so he puts the cloak on top of the king.

133
00:10:14,061 --> 00:10:16,060
The king wakes up and says, who put the coat on me?

134
00:10:16,061 --> 00:10:17,960
And the crown keeper says, I did.

135
00:10:17,961 --> 00:10:23,360
And the king immediately executes the crown keeper because that was not his job.

136
00:10:23,361 --> 00:10:28,060
These principles guided the Qin armies in the third century BC, when according to a

137
00:10:28,061 --> 00:10:30,180
a Han Dynasty historian, they began

138
00:10:30,181 --> 00:10:32,020
moving across the map of China,

139
00:10:32,460 --> 00:10:33,820
absorbing the other states

140
00:10:33,821 --> 00:10:35,800
as a silkworm devours

141
00:10:35,801 --> 00:10:37,160
a mulberry leaf.

142
00:10:38,360 --> 00:10:39,600
In 246 BC,

143
00:10:39,860 --> 00:10:42,200
a pivotal event of enormous significance

144
00:10:42,201 --> 00:10:44,660
occurred. A 13-year-old

145
00:10:44,661 --> 00:10:46,240
boy ascended to the throne

146
00:10:46,241 --> 00:10:48,180
of Qin. He was to become

147
00:10:48,181 --> 00:10:50,440
known as Qin Shu Huangdi,

148
00:10:50,441 --> 00:10:52,440
the first sovereign emperor

149
00:10:52,441 --> 00:10:53,380
of China.

150
00:10:54,280 --> 00:10:56,180
Legend says he flew to the moon

151
00:10:56,181 --> 00:10:57,920
on a magic carpet in a dream.

152
00:10:58,060 --> 00:11:02,660
Looking down, he saw his kingdom was vulnerable to his enemies, who were many,

153
00:11:03,120 --> 00:11:05,480
and at the mercy of invaders from all sides.

154
00:11:05,920 --> 00:11:08,320
He roused his advisors from sleep and announced,

155
00:11:08,600 --> 00:11:10,380
I will build a great wall.

156
00:11:12,400 --> 00:11:16,960
The original Chinese symbol for the word country depicted a walled enclosure.

157
00:11:17,520 --> 00:11:22,460
By 500 BC, the symbol had evolved to include a soldier within the enclosure.

158
00:11:23,140 --> 00:11:24,780
Modern Marvels will return.

159
00:11:24,781 --> 00:11:31,780
We now return to the Great Wall of China on Modern Marvels.

160
00:11:32,500 --> 00:11:38,600
In 1974, farmers digging a well uncovered an astonishing archaeological find.

161
00:11:39,340 --> 00:11:43,780
A cache of terracotta soldiers, bowmen, chariots and horses,

162
00:11:44,460 --> 00:11:49,360
all life-size and each one different, as if modeled from life.

163
00:11:50,140 --> 00:11:53,200
To date, more than 6,000 figures have been excavated.

164
00:11:53,201 --> 00:11:59,060
an entire terracotta army designed to fight the battles of a great king in the next life,

165
00:11:59,400 --> 00:12:02,420
or perhaps guard his eternal rest in his realm.

166
00:12:03,420 --> 00:12:09,940
Nearby lay the tomb of China's first emperor, Qin Shu Wang Di, known as the only first.

167
00:12:11,760 --> 00:12:14,660
According to accounts handed down by later dynasties,

168
00:12:15,120 --> 00:12:19,620
Qin Shu Wang Di's claim to the throne of Qin was of dubious origin.

169
00:12:19,621 --> 00:12:25,460
His mother was an enthralling young dancer and concubine to a prosperous traveling merchant

170
00:12:25,461 --> 00:12:27,940
who was as cunning as he was wealthy.

171
00:12:28,800 --> 00:12:33,540
While trading at a royal court, the merchant asked her to dance for the young Qin heir.

172
00:12:34,680 --> 00:12:39,680
When the princely heir fell in love with her, the merchant gave the concubine to him,

173
00:12:40,100 --> 00:12:44,380
never mentioning that she was already pregnant with his own child.

174
00:12:45,220 --> 00:12:47,720
The prince died after a short reign,

175
00:12:48,280 --> 00:12:52,400
and Shinshu Wangdi ascended to the throne while still a child.

176
00:12:53,260 --> 00:12:58,360
As the boy king matured, he began to show signs of eccentric behavior and paranoia.

177
00:12:59,000 --> 00:13:01,180
He banished his mother and ordered his father,

178
00:13:01,300 --> 00:13:05,040
the former merchant who had ruled during his boyhood, to commit suicide.

179
00:13:05,480 --> 00:13:10,160
He surrounded himself with soothsayers, sorcerers and necromancers,

180
00:13:10,620 --> 00:13:12,620
and shrewd, unscrupulous advisors.

181
00:13:13,540 --> 00:13:18,220
Around 234 B.C., he sent his army out to complete the conquest of China.

182
00:13:18,640 --> 00:13:20,440
His Qin predecessors had begun.

183
00:13:22,100 --> 00:13:26,080
By the warring states period, you have independent states slugging it out.

184
00:13:26,660 --> 00:13:31,860
And finally, only five are left, only four are left, only three are left, and only one is left.

185
00:13:32,360 --> 00:13:34,620
And in 221 B.C., China is unified.

186
00:13:35,040 --> 00:13:39,440
When the state of Qin destroys the state of Qin...

187
00:13:39,441 --> 00:13:48,860
The king declared himself first sovereign emperor of a new land he named China after his dynasty and moved quickly to consolidate his power.

188
00:13:49,740 --> 00:13:55,874
Chin Shih Huangdi really did create an empire where there had not been one before.

189
00:13:55,875 --> 00:14:01,783
He built a national road or highway system. He standardized the Chinese script.

190
00:14:01,784 --> 00:14:05,972
He unified Chinese currency. He standardized the empire.

191
00:14:05,973 --> 00:14:08,440
He made it one big single entity.

192
00:14:08,441 --> 00:14:13,200
and coupled with that was this incredible interest in magic and alchemy

193
00:14:13,201 --> 00:14:19,900
and the belief that somehow he could defy all mortal bounds and live forever.

194
00:14:21,000 --> 00:14:22,320
He was a megalomaniac.

195
00:14:23,800 --> 00:14:27,500
Chin-Shu Wang-Di standardized the kingdom's weights and measurements

196
00:14:27,501 --> 00:14:31,320
with a system based on the number six, his magic number.

197
00:14:32,420 --> 00:14:34,540
He declared black his mystical color,

198
00:14:34,860 --> 00:14:38,240
the official color of all clothing, flags and pendants of the realm.

199
00:14:38,440 --> 00:14:41,560
and elevated his title to that of the only first,

200
00:14:42,120 --> 00:14:45,240
thus decreeing that the Qin dynasty would rule forever.

201
00:14:46,320 --> 00:14:48,960
Then he decided to build a great wall.

202
00:14:58,280 --> 00:15:01,900
No one knows exactly when the notion of building a great wall

203
00:15:01,901 --> 00:15:05,500
came into the emperor's mind, or why he decided to build it.

204
00:15:06,420 --> 00:15:13,100
One legend has it that one of his soothsayers predicted his dynasty would fall at the hands of a northern barbarian tribe.

205
00:15:13,820 --> 00:15:21,480
Others relate to dreams, omens, and the emperor's inclination to build prodigious monuments to his own glory whenever possible.

206
00:15:21,740 --> 00:15:25,000
We do know that around the time he unified China,

207
00:15:25,500 --> 00:15:30,940
Xinshu Wangdi placed General Meng Tian, an energetic and successful military officer,

208
00:15:30,941 --> 00:15:33,500
in charge of building a great wall

209
00:15:33,501 --> 00:15:37,300
that would separate the civilized people and fertile lands of China

210
00:15:37,301 --> 00:15:41,800
from the barbarians and demons said to inhabit the barren steppes to the north.

211
00:15:42,300 --> 00:15:46,720
The wall was to stretch from the Yellow Sea in the east to the Gobi Desert in the west.

212
00:15:47,360 --> 00:15:49,560
It was to be 24 feet high in places

213
00:15:49,561 --> 00:15:53,620
and wide enough for eight men to march abreast along its top.

214
00:15:54,140 --> 00:15:57,640
The wall was to follow natural features of the land whenever possible

215
00:15:57,641 --> 00:16:03,120
and never to flow in a straight line because demons were believed to travel only in straight lines

216
00:16:03,121 --> 00:16:06,860
and were therefore unable to cross crooked lines or turn corners.

217
00:16:09,560 --> 00:16:14,060
The construction of the wall varied greatly in technique from place to place

218
00:16:14,061 --> 00:16:20,020
and very little remains of Qin Shu Wang Di's wall to tell us how it was done,

219
00:16:20,420 --> 00:16:25,740
though many scholars believe it served as a pattern for future walls built on its foundation.

220
00:16:26,680 --> 00:16:29,960
Meng Tian probably began with the towers.

221
00:16:30,980 --> 00:16:33,840
Made of brick or stone with a stone or rubble base,

222
00:16:34,220 --> 00:16:38,020
they were generally about 40 feet high and 40 feet square at the base,

223
00:16:38,440 --> 00:16:40,560
tapering to 30 feet square at the summit.

224
00:16:41,540 --> 00:16:45,020
Once the towers were complete, they were joined by a stone curtain,

225
00:16:45,620 --> 00:16:49,520
a demon barrier, to keep out invaders and evil spirits.

226
00:16:51,420 --> 00:16:54,560
Garrison towers large enough to hold hundreds of troops

227
00:16:54,561 --> 00:16:57,500
were stationed the length of two arrow shots apart,

228
00:16:58,060 --> 00:17:00,820
so that all of the territory in between was covered.

229
00:17:01,600 --> 00:17:03,920
The towers protruded from the wall like turrets

230
00:17:03,921 --> 00:17:07,140
so defenders could fire at attackers along the wall's base.

231
00:17:07,880 --> 00:17:10,460
It is estimated that the Qin under General Meng

232
00:17:10,461 --> 00:17:13,060
constructed several hundred miles of new wall.

233
00:17:13,680 --> 00:17:16,940
The rest of the barrier was made up of already existing walls,

234
00:17:16,941 --> 00:17:20,340
which were joined and incorporated into its body.

235
00:17:20,341 --> 00:17:27,240
and essentially what he was doing was linking a series of earlier walls which the warring states

236
00:17:27,241 --> 00:17:32,100
had already built against the nomads in the north i would say they absolutely followed the rammed

237
00:17:32,101 --> 00:17:37,400
earth technique it's the only technique they knew they may have done it bigger or taller wider but

238
00:17:37,401 --> 00:17:43,460
it's no different in construction techniques from the neolithic walls in the eastern mountains dry

239
00:17:43,461 --> 00:17:49,880
earth was pounded between two wall faces of brick or stone until it was of a sufficient level and

240
00:17:49,881 --> 00:17:50,581
solid.

241
00:17:50,582 --> 00:17:56,080
Then a layer of brick or stone was laid on top to seal it against rain and make a roadway.

242
00:17:58,080 --> 00:18:03,040
Further west, where the land is made of a fine silt called yellow earth or los,

243
00:18:03,480 --> 00:18:09,500
laborers poured the soil mixed with water into wooden frames and rammed it into a solid structure

244
00:18:09,501 --> 00:18:10,497
as it dried.

245
00:18:10,498 --> 00:18:17,140
On the arid western plain, the wall was built from layers of palm fronds, reeds,

246
00:18:17,141 --> 00:18:23,440
and gravel held together with mud, ending at the edges of the great desert, beyond which

247
00:18:23,441 --> 00:18:30,020
lay the demon-haunted wilderness known to the Chinese as Ku Wai, outside the passes.

248
00:18:32,560 --> 00:18:37,480
General Meng built the first great wall in less than 10 years, completing it sometime

249
00:18:37,481 --> 00:18:38,793
before 210 B.C.

250
00:18:38,793 --> 00:18:45,180
But already stories had begun to circulate, calculating its cost in terms

251
00:18:45,181 --> 00:18:47,600
of human suffering and human lives.

252
00:18:57,540 --> 00:19:01,580
The massive manpower necessary to build the Great Wall

253
00:19:01,581 --> 00:19:04,940
was recruited by forced conscription from the peasantry,

254
00:19:05,380 --> 00:19:08,260
supplemented with criminals, captured soldiers,

255
00:19:08,820 --> 00:19:10,580
defeated nobles, scholars,

256
00:19:10,581 --> 00:19:13,940
and other so-called enemies of the sovereign.

257
00:19:15,180 --> 00:19:19,660
It has been said that every third man in the empire was pressed into service,

258
00:19:20,140 --> 00:19:23,700
and of every ten recruited, only three returned home.

259
00:19:25,320 --> 00:19:29,780
Legend tells that the emperor ordered that any man found taking a nap on the wall

260
00:19:29,781 --> 00:19:32,440
should be buried alive in it.

261
00:19:34,020 --> 00:19:38,280
The popular memory of wall building is of peasants being rounded up,

262
00:19:38,400 --> 00:19:43,520
being sent off to work, never coming back, dying in some remote wilderness,

263
00:19:43,521 --> 00:19:46,980
slaving away. It's an absolutely horrible story.

264
00:19:46,981 --> 00:19:50,440
And the legend is that the bodies of workers who

265
00:19:50,441 --> 00:19:57,320
died were simply tossed into the middle part of the wall between the masonry where you just had

266
00:19:57,321 --> 00:19:58,021
rubble.

267
00:19:58,022 --> 00:20:03,300
This outrage or this popular feeling expressed itself in a variety of poems, but

268
00:20:03,301 --> 00:20:07,246
perhaps most famously in this legend of Lady Meng.

269
00:20:07,247 --> 00:20:10,560
One of the great stories that kids studied

270
00:20:10,561 --> 00:20:18,080
in school in the early first 20 years of communist rule was with a parable of this woman going to

271
00:20:18,081 --> 00:20:23,800
find her husband, who Qin Shi Huang had sent to the wall to work as a form of slave labor,

272
00:20:23,801 --> 00:20:28,703
and she finds that he's dead and probably buried in the wall, as many people were.

273
00:20:28,704 --> 00:20:29,720
So in that sense,

274
00:20:29,800 --> 00:20:37,840
the wall was viewed as an artifact of feudal oppression built by the sweat of ordinary people

275
00:20:37,841 --> 00:20:40,080
under the whiplash of this tyrant.

276
00:20:41,100 --> 00:20:43,320
Now, we find it's exactly the opposite.

277
00:20:44,100 --> 00:20:46,100
And the Great Wall has been transformed

278
00:20:46,101 --> 00:20:48,340
into this symbol of China's greatness,

279
00:20:48,920 --> 00:20:50,780
the longevity of its civilization,

280
00:20:51,500 --> 00:20:56,380
and representing a country of great power,

281
00:20:56,680 --> 00:20:58,040
history, and consequence.

282
00:20:58,780 --> 00:21:01,140
When construction fell behind schedule,

283
00:21:01,300 --> 00:21:03,020
one of his seers told the emperor

284
00:21:03,021 --> 00:21:04,840
the wall would never be completed

285
00:21:04,841 --> 00:21:08,080
unless 10,000 men were buried alive in it.

286
00:21:08,780 --> 00:21:11,340
Feeling he couldn't spare 10,000 workers,

287
00:21:11,860 --> 00:21:15,720
Chinchue Wangdi solved the problem by finding one man

288
00:21:15,721 --> 00:21:19,140
whose name included the Chinese character for 10,000

289
00:21:19,141 --> 00:21:21,560
and entombed him in the wall instead.

290
00:21:22,560 --> 00:21:25,000
It is estimated that more than a million men

291
00:21:25,001 --> 00:21:27,400
worked on the wall during its years of construction

292
00:21:27,401 --> 00:21:29,540
under deplorable conditions,

293
00:21:30,000 --> 00:21:32,160
and there can be no doubt that thousands of them

294
00:21:32,161 --> 00:21:34,620
died of exposure, exhaustion, and starvation.

295
00:21:34,840 --> 00:21:44,160
Even today, stories persist that their bodies were buried where they fell, interred forever inside the longest cemetery on earth.

296
00:21:46,480 --> 00:21:50,060
Less than a decade had passed since the unification of China.

297
00:21:50,640 --> 00:21:52,520
Other vast changes had occurred.

298
00:21:53,200 --> 00:21:59,060
Great public works projects, canals, roads and agricultural programs had been successfully completed.

299
00:21:59,700 --> 00:22:01,640
Now with the Great Wall in place,

300
00:22:02,140 --> 00:22:05,820
Qin Shi Huangdi boasted that his empire was invincible.

301
00:22:06,580 --> 00:22:08,180
But the Chinese have a saying.

302
00:22:08,700 --> 00:22:11,860
When things have reached their peak, they decline.

303
00:22:14,740 --> 00:22:19,320
We now return to the Great Wall of China on Modern Marvels.

304
00:22:20,820 --> 00:22:23,640
Even as the Great Wall was under construction,

305
00:22:24,280 --> 00:22:28,640
Qin Shi Huangdi descended further into mysticism and madness.

306
00:22:29,060 --> 00:22:33,820
In 213 BC, he decided that history should begin with him

307
00:22:33,821 --> 00:22:38,300
and ordered that all copies of books of history and classical literature be burned.

308
00:22:38,980 --> 00:22:43,200
Any person found to be in possession of such books 30 days after the decree

309
00:22:43,201 --> 00:22:48,660
was to be shipped off to do forced labor on the Great Wall or be buried alive.

310
00:22:49,260 --> 00:22:52,120
An estimated 460 scholars perished.

311
00:22:52,920 --> 00:22:56,520
When the emperor's eldest son and heir disagreed with the policy,

312
00:22:56,521 --> 00:23:00,320
he too was exiled to the north to assist General Mun.

313
00:23:01,180 --> 00:23:05,420
As he grew older, the emperor's obsession with his own death intensified.

314
00:23:05,940 --> 00:23:10,220
Although he made only one recorded visit to survey the construction of his great wall,

315
00:23:10,680 --> 00:23:17,160
he reportedly made as many as five extensive journeys in search of an elixir of immortality.

316
00:23:18,060 --> 00:23:22,840
On one such journey in 210 BC, he died at the age of 49,

317
00:23:22,841 --> 00:23:29,620
his death possibly hastened by noxious potions containing mercury, arsenic, and sulfur,

318
00:23:30,220 --> 00:23:33,420
which he had consumed in his quest for eternal life.

319
00:23:34,220 --> 00:23:38,480
There isn't a great deal about him that you could find alike in the records that have come down to us.

320
00:23:38,481 --> 00:23:42,240
He burnt the books, he's supposed to have buried Confucians alive, and so on.

321
00:23:42,820 --> 00:23:46,160
It's very hard to find much that would be admirable, but he was a very powerful ruler.

322
00:23:46,820 --> 00:23:52,060
And like most rulers in ancient China, he wished to be buried as a king should be buried.

323
00:23:52,061 --> 00:23:56,460
And he started work on his tomb some 30 or 40 years before he died.

324
00:23:56,720 --> 00:24:00,080
Even before he united China, he had started work on this massive mausoleum.

325
00:24:00,980 --> 00:24:04,200
Excavation continues on the pits of terracotta soldiers,

326
00:24:04,720 --> 00:24:08,020
but Chinchou Wangdi's burial mound is yet to be opened.

327
00:24:08,480 --> 00:24:09,940
It is said to be booby-trapped,

328
00:24:10,280 --> 00:24:15,380
and is believed to contain treasures more magnificent than those recovered from any other tomb,

329
00:24:15,880 --> 00:24:20,320
as well as the bodies of the emperor's wives and the architects who planned its design

330
00:24:20,321 --> 00:24:22,700
so they could never give away its secrets.

331
00:24:23,880 --> 00:24:27,480
The first emperor was told by a soothsayer

332
00:24:27,481 --> 00:24:32,240
that he was going to be overthrown by Hu, by H-U.

333
00:24:32,920 --> 00:24:35,740
And he took this to mean the Hu,

334
00:24:35,900 --> 00:24:39,100
which is the Chinese word for the horse nomads,

335
00:24:39,101 --> 00:24:40,280
for the barbarians.

336
00:24:40,440 --> 00:24:44,700
But his dynasty perished at the hands of his second son, Hu Hai.

337
00:24:45,440 --> 00:24:47,580
With the rightful heir still in exile,

338
00:24:47,581 --> 00:24:52,760
Hu Hai ascended to the throne of Qin with all of his father's viciousness and guile,

339
00:24:53,100 --> 00:24:55,540
but none of his strength and leadership abilities.

340
00:24:56,340 --> 00:25:00,780
He imprisoned all his father's advisors, including General Meng Tian,

341
00:25:01,400 --> 00:25:05,100
who, after reflection on his misfortunes, took his own life,

342
00:25:05,660 --> 00:25:11,020
saying that he deserved to die because he had violated the chi, or energy flow of the earth,

343
00:25:11,021 --> 00:25:16,720
by cutting through mountains, rivers, and other natural features when he built the Great Wall.

344
00:25:17,580 --> 00:25:24,580
Buhai ruled only four years before rebel forces deposed him and China was thrown into civil war.

345
00:25:24,581 --> 00:25:29,580
The great dynasty that was to last forever had lasted only 15 years,

346
00:25:29,581 --> 00:25:33,580
the shortest reign of any dynasty ever to rule China.

347
00:25:33,581 --> 00:25:42,580
For all its imposing size and strength, the Great Wall wasn't very effective in stemming the tide of invasions from northern nomads.

348
00:25:42,581 --> 00:25:43,281
nomads.

349
00:25:43,282 --> 00:25:50,800
Known collectively as Tartars, after a medieval name for hell, the Chinese said

350
00:25:50,801 --> 00:25:56,540
they were more monsters than men, devouring the flesh of dogs and horses and drinking

351
00:25:56,541 --> 00:26:02,700
the blood of their enemies, strong of body, ruthless of heart, and in war, invincible.

352
00:26:04,100 --> 00:26:09,240
One of them who was preparing an assault on the Great Wall was a fierce Mongolian leader

353
00:26:09,241 --> 00:26:14,460
whose name would become synonymous with terror throughout the empire and the world.

354
00:26:15,200 --> 00:26:16,080
Genghis Khan.

355
00:26:20,160 --> 00:26:23,240
If the Great Wall were transplanted to the United States,

356
00:26:23,320 --> 00:26:26,780
it would run a zigzag course from Philadelphia to Topeka, Kansas.

357
00:26:27,280 --> 00:26:30,920
The Great Wall of China will continue in a moment on Modern Marvels.

358
00:26:31,660 --> 00:26:36,160
We now return to the Great Wall of China on Modern Marvels.

359
00:26:36,161 --> 00:26:41,600
For more than a thousand years after the death of the first emperor,

360
00:26:42,160 --> 00:26:45,660
subsequent dynasties worked on the wall or allowed it to decay

361
00:26:45,661 --> 00:26:49,440
according to the military needs and political climate of the times.

362
00:26:50,380 --> 00:26:53,860
Some, like the Han dynasty, kept parts of the wall in repair,

363
00:26:54,360 --> 00:26:56,740
added to it, or built their own walls.

364
00:26:57,920 --> 00:27:02,680
Others, like the Tang dynasty, who believed it useless as a defensive barrier

365
00:27:02,681 --> 00:27:06,620
and too expensive to maintain, allowed it to fall into ruin.

366
00:27:07,800 --> 00:27:12,400
The barbarian onslaughts continued with most of the successful invaders

367
00:27:12,401 --> 00:27:14,760
gradually assimilating into the culture,

368
00:27:15,380 --> 00:27:18,680
often becoming more Chinese than the Chinese.

369
00:27:21,340 --> 00:27:24,740
Well, the popular conception of the Great Wall is that it was an enclosure

370
00:27:24,741 --> 00:27:30,600
that would keep the barbarians out and protect China from invasion.

371
00:27:30,601 --> 00:27:39,660
and that also made it difficult for Chinese to move into areas beyond the Great Wall.

372
00:27:40,300 --> 00:27:43,980
It was seen as the boundary between civilization and non-civilization.

373
00:27:44,800 --> 00:27:49,760
And the truth of the matter is the wall was very porous, very easily crossed.

374
00:27:50,760 --> 00:27:55,640
The idea that you can seal off the frontier is not going to work.

375
00:27:56,100 --> 00:27:59,780
The nomads will always find a way, they'll find a break in the wall.

376
00:27:59,781 --> 00:28:05,960
they will often simply be let in by people who are guarding the wall and even in the Ming,

377
00:28:05,961 --> 00:28:11,160
at the heyday of wall building, they regularly either went around the end of fortifications,

378
00:28:11,161 --> 00:28:16,180
came through gates and they turned up right outside the city walls of Peking.

379
00:28:16,181 --> 00:28:24,160
Legend says Genghis Khan was born clutching a clot of blood in his right fist as a symbol of his

380
00:28:24,161 --> 00:28:25,219
bloody future.

381
00:28:25,220 --> 00:28:31,500
As a young man, he conquered and united warring Mongol factions and forged a mighty

382
00:28:31,501 --> 00:28:32,201
army.

383
00:28:32,202 --> 00:28:40,140
And in 1211 AD, the Mongol horde of Genghis Khan swept down upon China like devils loosed out

384
00:28:40,141 --> 00:28:46,180
of hell, crossing the Great Wall and spreading across the land like grasshoppers covering the

385
00:28:46,181 --> 00:28:52,445
face of the earth. This force was able to create the largest empire in the world.

386
00:28:52,446 --> 00:28:53,760
I mean, they were

387
00:28:53,761 --> 00:28:59,040
unbelievably fierce fighters and they relied upon in addition to their

388
00:28:59,041 --> 00:29:05,340
martial skills a psychological terror when they took the city of Herat they

389
00:29:05,341 --> 00:29:10,040
killed everybody because they wanted to impress all of the people of Central

390
00:29:10,041 --> 00:29:13,360
Asia with the fact that if there was any resistance that opponents would be killed

391
00:29:13,361 --> 00:29:20,620
the greatest joy a man can have is victory to conquer one's enemies to

392
00:29:20,621 --> 00:29:28,220
pursue them, to deprive them of their possessions, to reduce their families to tears, to ride on their

393
00:29:28,221 --> 00:29:33,535
horses and to make love to their wives and daughters. Genghis Khan.

394
00:29:33,536 --> 00:29:35,360
It took 60 bloody years

395
00:29:35,361 --> 00:29:41,340
before the whole of China fell to the Mongols under the leadership of Genghis Khan's grandson,

396
00:29:41,341 --> 00:29:45,836
Kublai Khan. He declared himself emperor of China in 1271.

397
00:29:45,837 --> 00:29:48,860
The Mongols would rule China for nearly

398
00:29:48,861 --> 00:29:50,020
100 years.

399
00:29:54,900 --> 00:29:56,780
China under Mongol rule

400
00:29:56,781 --> 00:29:58,820
became a cosmopolitan place

401
00:29:58,821 --> 00:30:00,880
because the Mongol Empire opened

402
00:30:00,881 --> 00:30:02,940
China to Eurasia and connected

403
00:30:02,941 --> 00:30:04,880
it to the trade routes that crossed

404
00:30:04,881 --> 00:30:06,980
the land all the way to the Middle East.

405
00:30:08,000 --> 00:30:08,820
It was during

406
00:30:08,821 --> 00:30:10,800
this time that Marco Polo is said

407
00:30:10,801 --> 00:30:12,900
to have gone to China and it has long

408
00:30:12,901 --> 00:30:14,860
been debated as to why he never

409
00:30:14,861 --> 00:30:16,520
mentioned the Great Wall in his writings.

410
00:30:16,521 --> 00:30:24,620
In fact, it is likely that the Great Wall of China as we know it did not exist during Marco Polo's days at the court of the Khan.

411
00:30:27,380 --> 00:30:32,840
During the years of Mongol rule, what remained of the Qin Great Wall had fallen into ruin.

412
00:30:34,020 --> 00:30:40,800
The Chinese never accepted Mongol rule, and Kublai Khan was never regarded as anything but a barbarian by the Chinese,

413
00:30:41,020 --> 00:30:44,700
no matter how much he impressed Marco Polo and the other visitors.

414
00:30:45,600 --> 00:30:50,180
Frequent uprisings by Chinese rebels chipped away at the Mongol occupation

415
00:30:50,181 --> 00:30:54,860
until finally, in 1368, a Chinese peasant led an army

416
00:30:54,861 --> 00:30:57,860
which drove the last of the Mongols out of China

417
00:30:57,861 --> 00:31:00,540
and founded the Ming Dynasty.

418
00:31:07,300 --> 00:31:11,080
The Ming are the most active wall builders in Chinese history.

419
00:31:11,700 --> 00:31:13,620
If the Ming had never done their work,

420
00:31:13,621 --> 00:31:16,480
there would be no idea of a Great Wall of China,

421
00:31:16,960 --> 00:31:19,540
and the fortifications built by other dynasties

422
00:31:19,541 --> 00:31:22,160
would be interesting footnotes in historical texts.

423
00:31:23,040 --> 00:31:25,860
What we know today as the Great Wall is certainly the Ming Wall.

424
00:31:25,980 --> 00:31:28,400
That's what you see if you go to visit the Great Wall of China,

425
00:31:28,620 --> 00:31:31,600
and it's very different from any of its predecessors.

426
00:31:32,040 --> 00:31:34,920
It's made of masonry in most places.

427
00:31:35,200 --> 00:31:36,480
It has crenellations.

428
00:31:37,000 --> 00:31:40,020
To build the Ming Walls, you had to have specialized groups

429
00:31:40,021 --> 00:31:42,400
of masons and stonecutters and whatnot.

430
00:31:42,401 --> 00:31:44,420
You had to pay them with money.

431
00:31:45,140 --> 00:31:50,480
It's an operation of an entirely different scale and character compared to the earlier ones.

432
00:31:50,800 --> 00:32:00,380
Much of the Ming wall is built on a foundation of granite blocks as long as 14 feet and 3 or 4 feet thick, all accurately cut and fitted.

433
00:32:01,300 --> 00:32:07,900
A facing of handmade bricks up to 5 feet thick was built up on top of these blocks up to 20 feet in height.

434
00:32:07,901 --> 00:32:17,900
The center cavity was filled with pounded earth and covered with the same brick, forming a 14-foot wide roadway across the top.

435
00:32:19,900 --> 00:32:29,160
The Ming created elaborate gates along points in the wall and crowned the eastern terminus at Shan Hai Kwan with the first gate under heaven.

436
00:32:29,161 --> 00:32:39,360
At the western terminus at Jiayu Kwan, they built the Jade Gate or Marshall Barrier.

437
00:32:41,180 --> 00:32:47,620
The Ming left hundreds of memorials and tablets along the wall extolling the emperor's wisdom and virtues

438
00:32:47,621 --> 00:32:51,880
and providing interesting data about the work they had completed.

439
00:32:51,881 --> 00:33:03,167
One name that appears frequently is that of Wan Li, who reigned in the 16th century and made such a contribution to the Great Wall that many Chinese in later centuries

440
00:33:03,168 --> 00:33:05,600
thought he was the original builder.

441
00:33:06,640 --> 00:33:15,860
The Chinese name for the wall, Wan Li Changcheng, meaning Wall of 10,000 Li, can also be translated as Wan Li's Wall.

442
00:33:15,861 --> 00:33:25,920
But as powerful as Wanli's wall was, it couldn't keep the Mongols from making regular raids into Chinese territory

443
00:33:25,921 --> 00:33:30,520
Or block the armies of a powerful tribe of hunters from Manchuria

444
00:33:30,521 --> 00:33:35,220
Who cast covetous eyes on the rich lands to the south

445
00:33:35,221 --> 00:33:49,221
The Ming obsession with the Great Wall became their psychological defense against the Mongols.

446
00:33:49,640 --> 00:33:53,060
It wasn't much of a military defense in itself.

447
00:33:53,640 --> 00:33:59,140
It created a kind of Star Wars mentality that somehow they would be able to, at great expense and cost,

448
00:33:59,240 --> 00:34:05,140
find a way to hold off the Mongols, who had been expelled from China but never brought under control.

449
00:34:05,220 --> 00:34:13,960
One of the striking things about the Great Wall is it's very expensive to maintain troops there, to supply troops.

450
00:34:14,620 --> 00:34:21,040
The estimate is if you send 60 cartloads of grain up to the Great Wall,

451
00:34:21,300 --> 00:34:24,440
you have to eat 59 of them to get that one cartload up there.

452
00:34:24,940 --> 00:34:29,760
This means that the Chinese tended to establish military colonies at the wall itself,

453
00:34:29,840 --> 00:34:32,220
where the soldiers would be farmers too and would feed themselves.

454
00:34:32,221 --> 00:34:36,900
The Chinese who were sent to garrison the area are very unhappy about it.

455
00:34:36,901 --> 00:34:38,060
They're sent there for life.

456
00:34:38,061 --> 00:34:39,420
They tend to settle down.

457
00:34:39,421 --> 00:34:44,700
So you find that there are plenty of Chinese up there who can speak nomadic languages,

458
00:34:44,701 --> 00:34:46,620
and they're on good terms with the Mongols.

459
00:34:46,621 --> 00:34:48,540
They trade, they make money.

460
00:34:48,541 --> 00:34:52,420
And it doesn't surprise me a bit that if some Mongols want to come through some gate

461
00:34:52,421 --> 00:34:57,340
that these people are supposed to be guarding, that, you know, in consideration of a small payment,

462
00:34:57,341 --> 00:34:59,180
then they will look the other way.

463
00:34:59,181 --> 00:35:10,040
This system was one of the foibles that led to the downfall of the Ming because they weren't very good farmers and the land up there wasn't very good.

464
00:35:10,380 --> 00:35:18,560
The system broke down and the military colonies themselves by the end of the Ming began to become barbarian themselves.

465
00:35:19,220 --> 00:35:28,820
The Manchurians took Peking in 1644 after the commanding officer at Nanku Pass and many of his troops deserted to join the Manchu.

466
00:35:29,180 --> 00:35:35,860
They would rule China into the beginning of the 20th century, when the last Manchu emperor,

467
00:35:35,861 --> 00:35:44,820
a child named Pu Yi, was overthrown by a democratic rebellion in 1912.

468
00:35:44,821 --> 00:35:51,680
It was a great source of humiliation that China twice was attacked and conquered by

469
00:35:51,681 --> 00:35:55,620
these barbarians from outside the wall, the Mongols and then the Manchus.

470
00:35:55,621 --> 00:36:01,700
And then interestingly enough, the name Barbarian then got applied to Westerners who suddenly

471
00:36:01,701 --> 00:36:03,260
arrived in ships.

472
00:36:03,261 --> 00:36:05,860
And of course there was no wall in the ocean.

473
00:36:05,861 --> 00:36:10,580
There was an interim where the Great Wall was a forgotten piece of history in a way to

474
00:36:10,581 --> 00:36:11,580
Chinese.

475
00:36:11,581 --> 00:36:14,400
It had stopped being functional.

476
00:36:14,401 --> 00:36:16,500
The fox had gotten into the hen house.

477
00:36:16,501 --> 00:36:19,220
It was basically all over.

478
00:36:19,221 --> 00:36:23,980
The Manchu didn't need the Great Wall as a military or national boundary because they

479
00:36:23,981 --> 00:36:26,400
They ruled the territory on both sides of it.

480
00:36:26,940 --> 00:36:29,160
It was designed to turn back barbarians.

481
00:36:29,500 --> 00:36:32,120
Yet now, the barbarians ruled China.

482
00:36:32,980 --> 00:36:39,400
The Great Wall of China would not play a significant role in Chinese history again for more than 300 years,

483
00:36:39,401 --> 00:36:48,760
until the 20th century, when it would be transformed from an emblem of China's guarded past into a powerful symbol for change.

484
00:36:48,761 --> 00:36:56,460
We now return to the Great Wall of China on Modern Marvels

485
00:36:56,461 --> 00:37:00,940
By the late 19th and early 20th centuries

486
00:37:00,941 --> 00:37:06,240
China had become a fashionable destination for the more adventurous Western tourists

487
00:37:06,241 --> 00:37:10,400
And no trip was complete without an outing to the Great Wall

488
00:37:10,401 --> 00:37:14,160
When the Westerners first came to China

489
00:37:14,161 --> 00:37:17,840
They were terribly impressed, I think, with the high degree of civilization they saw

490
00:37:18,640 --> 00:37:21,960
The cities were prosperous, lively, the population was immense.

491
00:37:22,420 --> 00:37:29,220
The elites were very refined, educated, a wonderful literate tradition going back thousands of years.

492
00:37:29,820 --> 00:37:33,340
And I think there was a tendency to glorify many of the achievements of Chinese civilization.

493
00:37:33,341 --> 00:37:39,520
And this Great Wall just outside of Beijing was another one of these remarkable achievements.

494
00:37:40,180 --> 00:37:44,940
While Westerners flocked to admire the Great Wall that had captured their imaginations,

495
00:37:44,941 --> 00:37:50,680
the Chinese still viewed it as a reminder of feudal oppression and military failure.

496
00:37:52,000 --> 00:37:56,580
In desert regions where shelter is hard to come by and building materials scarce,

497
00:37:57,240 --> 00:38:01,580
peasants carted away great sections of the wall for use as building materials,

498
00:38:01,840 --> 00:38:05,880
and other families had built dwellings inside of the ruin itself.

499
00:38:07,180 --> 00:38:10,740
What's interesting to observe is that when Westerners come and start visiting,

500
00:38:10,741 --> 00:38:13,360
the Chinese are just not interested at all.

501
00:38:13,361 --> 00:38:16,220
They pay no attention to it, and the Westerners are thrilled,

502
00:38:16,380 --> 00:38:20,100
and they get out and start measuring it, sketching it, drawing it.

503
00:38:20,220 --> 00:38:22,340
And half of this picture is still true today.

504
00:38:22,440 --> 00:38:26,000
That is, when Westerners get there, they still get all excited and start taking pictures.

505
00:38:27,400 --> 00:38:30,440
But today, what's fascinating is the Chinese do the same thing.

506
00:38:31,580 --> 00:38:35,740
During the Japanese invasion of China during the 30s and 40s,

507
00:38:36,120 --> 00:38:39,840
many of the war's great battles were fought along the Great Wall,

508
00:38:39,841 --> 00:38:43,760
and it began to be portrayed as a symbol of popular resistance,

509
00:38:44,300 --> 00:38:46,320
particularly among the communists.

510
00:38:47,400 --> 00:38:51,120
Mao Zedong himself and many other leaders of the communist movement

511
00:38:51,121 --> 00:38:54,380
celebrated the Great Wall in poetry and song.

512
00:38:55,060 --> 00:38:59,200
In fact, the song which is now the national anthem of the People's Republic of China

513
00:38:59,201 --> 00:39:02,520
was written by a communist poet in 1935.

514
00:39:03,800 --> 00:39:06,380
Arise ye who refuse to be slaves,

515
00:39:06,381 --> 00:39:10,400
with our very flesh and blood let us build our new Great Wall.

516
00:39:11,280 --> 00:39:14,200
After the Communist Revolution in 1949,

517
00:39:14,920 --> 00:39:17,360
there was a brief renaissance for the Great Wall

518
00:39:17,361 --> 00:39:20,380
and restoration work was begun along portions

519
00:39:20,381 --> 00:39:22,420
that had been badly damaged in the war.

520
00:39:23,340 --> 00:39:26,720
But the Communists' regard for the Great Wall of China

521
00:39:26,721 --> 00:39:28,120
was to be short-lived.

522
00:39:31,420 --> 00:39:33,380
Mao Zedong was an incredible paradox.

523
00:39:33,381 --> 00:39:42,380
He was really sort of deeply, I think, riven between being very organically connected to China's past

524
00:39:42,381 --> 00:39:48,380
and also having an ideology which required, in a sense, that he'd be opposed to it.

525
00:39:48,381 --> 00:39:52,380
So there was a great contradiction at work at the heart of Chairman Mao.

526
00:39:52,381 --> 00:39:56,380
And in a certain sense, the Great Wall lies at the heart of it.

527
00:39:56,381 --> 00:40:00,380
It was a symbol of China's unity and greatness.

528
00:40:00,381 --> 00:40:07,140
It was also a symbol of its autocracy, of its sort of wanton oppression of ordinary people.

529
00:40:07,660 --> 00:40:08,840
He never worked this out.

530
00:40:09,780 --> 00:40:16,480
During Chairman Mao's great cultural revolution, many of China's treasures of the past were destroyed.

531
00:40:17,180 --> 00:40:20,420
Priceless bronze works of art were melted down for scrap.

532
00:40:20,421 --> 00:40:27,340
The magnificent wall surrounding the city of Beijing was torn down and a freeway built in its place.

533
00:40:28,180 --> 00:40:32,260
Thousands of ancient structures were demolished to make way for modern construction,

534
00:40:32,800 --> 00:40:35,160
and the Great Wall was no exception.

535
00:40:36,200 --> 00:40:38,860
Hundreds of miles of the Great Wall were destroyed,

536
00:40:39,080 --> 00:40:41,180
often with dynamite and heavy machinery,

537
00:40:41,181 --> 00:40:45,700
and the material used for road, reservoir, and building construction.

538
00:40:46,540 --> 00:40:49,060
But even as the Great Wall was being plundered,

539
00:40:49,540 --> 00:40:53,680
there was a quiet resurgence of respect for traditional Chinese culture,

540
00:40:53,681 --> 00:40:58,720
and a deepening sense of national identity rooted in Chinese history.

541
00:41:00,880 --> 00:41:06,180
Richard Nixon's 1972 visit to the Great Wall marked a symbolic turning point.

542
00:41:08,100 --> 00:41:13,460
When China was an empire, protocol dictated that visiting foreign dignitaries

543
00:41:13,461 --> 00:41:18,040
go to the palace and pay their respects by bowing down in front of the emperor.

544
00:41:19,100 --> 00:41:21,500
Today, there's no longer an emperor.

545
00:41:21,501 --> 00:41:28,080
but when official visitors come to China, the traditional ceremony of paying homage to the greatness of the country

546
00:41:28,081 --> 00:41:33,480
and to the Chinese people is often carried out with a trip to the Great Wall.

547
00:41:36,560 --> 00:41:41,140
The Great Wall in today's China has become a symbol of China's greatness,

548
00:41:41,700 --> 00:41:44,980
of the patriotic love of the Chinese people for their homeland,

549
00:41:45,680 --> 00:41:49,480
and of China's strength and determination to be respected.

550
00:41:51,500 --> 00:41:55,000
When the People's Republic of China joined the United Nations,

551
00:41:55,001 --> 00:42:00,000
they presented the organization with this spectacular tapestry of the Great Wall,

552
00:42:00,001 --> 00:42:05,000
measuring 32 by 16 feet and weighing 600 pounds,

553
00:42:05,001 --> 00:42:11,000
thus declaring the Great Wall the official symbol of the Chinese nation.

554
00:42:11,001 --> 00:42:18,000
In 1984, the Chinese government embarked on an active campaign of wall restoration,

555
00:42:18,001 --> 00:42:21,380
Launched by Premier Deng Xiaoping himself, declaring,

556
00:42:22,040 --> 00:42:25,820
let us love our country and restore our Great Wall.

557
00:42:27,180 --> 00:42:34,500
Since then, more than 200 world leaders and 50 million tourists have visited the Great Wall of China.

558
00:42:38,640 --> 00:42:43,200
It is an extraordinarily powerful tourist destination.

559
00:42:43,201 --> 00:42:49,520
In many ways, I mean, the Great Wall has become a kind of a, you know, Disneyland ride.

560
00:42:50,020 --> 00:42:53,100
You go there and you can sit on a camel and have your picture taken.

561
00:42:53,240 --> 00:42:55,120
You can dress up like Qin Shi Huang.

562
00:42:55,520 --> 00:42:58,260
Or you can buy, you know, tote bags.

563
00:42:58,680 --> 00:43:00,180
You can buy T-shirts.

564
00:43:00,580 --> 00:43:02,300
You know, I visited the Great Wall.

565
00:43:03,100 --> 00:43:04,560
Anywhere there's a little niche.

566
00:43:04,780 --> 00:43:06,060
Somebody has got a product.

567
00:43:06,061 --> 00:43:13,060
The Great Wall's popularity has prompted the Chinese to expand their restoration efforts

568
00:43:13,061 --> 00:43:19,060
to sites outside the major tourist area near Beijing so that crowding and associated wear and tear

569
00:43:19,061 --> 00:43:23,060
will not endanger the physical survival of the ruins.

570
00:43:23,061 --> 00:43:29,060
Nobody goes to China without going to the Great Wall. It's pretty spectacular.

571
00:43:29,061 --> 00:43:39,060
You rise up onto this wall that doesn't just sort of stretch away like some sort of modern-day construction,

572
00:43:39,061 --> 00:43:44,060
but it follows the contours of the hills and the mountains and it winds.

573
00:43:44,061 --> 00:43:51,060
And it is very much like some sort of a serpent that's coiled out across this very wild and uninhabited landscape.

574
00:43:51,061 --> 00:43:54,060
And it just sort of takes your breath away.

575
00:43:54,061 --> 00:43:57,760
Everybody knows the Great Wall.

576
00:43:58,700 --> 00:44:00,040
You don't need to have read a book.

577
00:44:00,760 --> 00:44:03,400
You don't need to have been very worldly.

578
00:44:04,140 --> 00:44:06,960
You think of China, what do you think of the Great Wall?

579
00:44:07,920 --> 00:44:10,220
The story of the Great Wall of China

580
00:44:10,221 --> 00:44:13,340
is one of bloody conflict and human suffering,

581
00:44:13,341 --> 00:44:18,600
which emerges ultimately as a tribute to the spirit of a land and a people.

582
00:44:19,400 --> 00:44:22,220
And as China integrates itself into the world community

583
00:44:22,221 --> 00:44:26,420
and rejects the path of isolation which the Wall so long symbolized.

584
00:44:26,860 --> 00:44:31,920
The Great Wall has been transformed into a marvel of persistence and antiquity,

585
00:44:32,540 --> 00:44:35,600
imperial splendor, and national pride.

586
00:44:41,600 --> 00:44:44,420
Ever wonder how the West was truly won?

